ky to use as it  comes with some situational applications  this is because the crystal actually  takes a bit of time to do its thing  after air sends it out when you hit  triangle the crystal travels to the  target point and if it connects will do  a small amount of damage if then takes a  couple of seconds to build some power  and explodes dishing out damage to  whoever and whatever is hanging around  it's more of an area of effect spell  than a direct attack so you'll need to  plan it carefully when using the longer  you hold triangle down the more powerful  the explosion will be when it happens  the best time to use Tempest is when  you've hit an enemy with a spell that  locks down their movement or they've  been staggered and thus unable to get  away or if they're being absolutely  pummeled by one of your teammates and  can't do anything but take the deadly  crystals to the face you'll want to use  that bad-boy abilities at the start of  the game your party members will have  limited access to weapons however you  will eventually be able to purchase new  ones or find them out in the world each  one has a cool look and can be leveled  up to become a devastating tool of  destruction as well as bolster your  character's core attributes another  major addition each weapon offers is a  new combat ability every weapon has a  special action that you can execute when  it's equipped however if you look at it  on the weapons leg menu you'll notice  there's a little bar that shows your  proficiency in its special action as you  put the weapon to use that bar will fill  and eventually the associate character  will gain that ability which can be used  wherever you have the weapon equipped or  not that proficiency bar fills up quite  slowly but if you pay attention to the  little bit of text underneath the bar  it'll tell you how you can speed the  process up trust me when I say it's  worth spending a bit of time grinding  whenever you get a new weapon so you can  max out the proficiency bar as quickly  as possible you want to do it with  random enemy mobs that you're familiar  with as you'll need to switch to  specific characters you're working on to  fulfil the objective and it's easier if  you know there won't be any surprises  some weapons are designed to present a  different style of play for the  Associated user and this style may not  vibe with the way you want to play them  so it's best to earn your proficiency in  low stakes bowels you don't want to be  facing a boss with weapons that don't  let you  your characters the way you expect them  out to be chapter select as previously  mentioned this article and in the Final  Fantasy remake review you should be  doing all the side quests you can they  enhance the story and can often give you  valuable items however if you rather  focus on getting the main campaign  finished and don't want to miss out on  anything  you'll be happy to hear that you can  come back and tie up loose ends Final  Fantasy 7 remake has a new game+ of  sorts it's actually a chapter select  that pops up after you've complete the  story and lets you drop in and out of  different chapters while keeping your  experience items and loadouts  the thing to know is that you'll start  at the very beginning of the chapter and  you'll need to complete the chapter to  retain the progress you made within it  so you may need to spend a bit of time  replaying chunks of the game to get to  the bit you want another lesson for life  on the ground floor there you have it a  few essential tips that Final Fantasy 7  doesn't tell you upfront hopefully you  haven't been stressed out by any of this  and if you were don't worry it's all  good you can enjoy the game now make  sure to watch our Final Fantasy 7 remake  video review here on YouTube and if you  have more tips let us know in the  comments below or hit me up on Twitter  at 2 more H  [Music]  you

